<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dodecanese campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> campaign was a British attempt in late 1943 to seize the Italian-held Dodecanese islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. British forces landed on Kos, Leros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> stayed in German hands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with air superiority, took Kos in October and Leros in November 1943, and the campaign ended as a costly Allied defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dodecanese-campaign.jpg" alt="Dodecanese campaign" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nomos_Dodekanisou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> had been under Italian control since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> of 1911. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, the largest island, was a military and air base, and Leros, with its deep-water port of Portolago, was developed into a fortified air base that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> called the Corregidor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. An earlier British attempt on the islands, Operation Abstention in February 1941, failed when Italian forces recaptured Kastellorizo from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> and No. 50 Commando.</p><p>The British Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> hoped that action in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> would help draw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> into the war, which would turn the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> flank in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and open another supply route to the USSR. Germany, which had foreseen an Italian defection, prepared Unternehmen Achse (Operation Axis) to disarm Italian troops wherever they were.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cassibile">Armistice of Cassibile</a> was announced on 8 September 1943, a British mission reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, but the German commander attacked the Italian garrison, and Admiral Inigo Campioni ordered the Italians there to surrender on 11 September. A Special Boat Service detachment landed on Kos on 14 September, and six Spitfire Mk Vs of 7 Squadron SAAF flew in to its airfield at Antimachia.</p><p>On 3 October the Germans made amphibious and airborne landings on Kos, code-named Unternehmen Eisbär (Operation Polar Bear). The British surrendered the next day, and the Germans took 1,388 British and 3,145 Italian prisoners. On 4 October German troops killed the Italian commander of the island, Colonnello Felice Leggio, and nearly 100 of his officers in the Massacre of Kos. Leros, held by most of the 234th Infantry Brigade and some 8,500 Italians under Ammiraglio Luigi Mascherpa, fell next. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> was then evacuated, and its Italian garrison surrendered on 22 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians count the campaign among the last British defeats and the last German victories of the war, and some have called it a badly planned fiasco. German air superiority decided it. About 4,800 British troops were killed or captured, six destroyers were sunk and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> lost 115 aircraft.</p><p>The German occupation also ended the relative safety of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the islands. In July 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> rounded up 1,700 members of the ancient Jewish community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and only some 160 of them survived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>. The islands stayed in German hands until the end of the war in 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dodecanese campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
